106291/selection/332b94f44851a83f86c54803c169aef12b2cb00138d1edbca2e74e0f33591183" "q-106291/selection/77e84e78369ca7372b4a9810f19782f0f5b5fdaf13a471e2bbddfc89e977e174"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106291" :question/id #uuid "f7c4811d-0136-5556-a7aa-255ad6fca4d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-106291" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/3c07f3e732084327f02849cee9355a46ce7a1c5f905d509b1e1404915612c85c.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,-2)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(6,-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,-2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-106291/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106291"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-106294/selection/254f7dc7c93945f5d800c08002268b921573aada24b982a69940059f541d28cc" :answer/id #uuid "00a27d6b-22c3-579d-b3ec-42f2193f261a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106294/selection/4205b99be199dc7950356b855ff4a5477c48c73878a7b026d25c035dbf46acb5" :answer/id #uuid "eb18ed28-75b8-5cb9-9a2d-bcc8cac4816f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106294/selection/5bda35dc7fabc5f84da2118d36bbe7c90786c71810c1d882ae9f6c6935b490ae" :answer/id #uuid "49ac2e7b-4a68-5a26-a6d2-ec6118fc43cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106294/selection/bc3e76ff6c2251409d67bd4d44714df545c2e1406f35c243db21f791e2a3cb52" :answer/id #uuid "45eb8ebc-8ae6-5592-a165-3375aebf28cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106294/selection/c9d3abca692a831eeb03f55b6843f1a875afc5b27a1e9401902341cdf4f618a6" :answer/id #uuid "6f40633a-bc89-5a92-990b-bf696c8fb8c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106294/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "db898e70-56f9-5772-9f9c-eb4a65e7b37b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-106294/selection/4205b99be199dc7950356b855ff4a5477c48c73878a7b026d25c035dbf46acb5" :answer-field/choices ["q-106294/selection/254f7dc7c93945f5d800c08002268b921573aada24b982a69940059f541d28cc" "q-106294/selection/4205b99be199dc7950356b855ff4a5477c48c73878a7b026d25c035dbf46acb5" "q-106294/selection/5bda35dc7fabc5f84da2118d36bbe7c90786c71810c1d882ae9f6c6935b490ae" "q-106294/selection/bc3e76ff6c2251409d67bd4d44714df545c2e1406f35c243db21f791e2a3cb52" "q-106294/selection/c9d3abca692a831eeb03f55b6843f1a875afc5b27a1e9401902341cdf4f618a6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106294" :question/id #uuid "9112bb74-acfb-590c-854b-03b69cc3e291" :question/math-academy-id "q-106294" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/eb1ef0ba48e294464496a901e6c593cb5e2bc215c688862e2b7eda3621f72469.png)\n\nThe semi-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/6d73d4841b5e8ca1c3996d1996732b71245f2673e0e76a50a008600b95cfaf09.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the required semi-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-106294/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106294"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-321948/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "9601c170-7b2e-5c66-9342-9a6a3b9dfb3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321948/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "dd8ff7a2-0f61-525d-a304-ad1c14eb6d8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321948/field-1" :answer-field/id #u