\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/682f73ccec945de2789ecca71aa6dae650c485189c60ffc64ab7a7aa108665da.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-321948/field-1" "q-321948/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-321948"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79210/selection/9d807895a62ccaaa6b0f7870d94a0d6b4337ff0af504bf4b2897ef85a13c3072" :answer/id #uuid "febd70cf-e59b-5da6-973f-162d74b42651" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79210/selection/25eef7f15a6e5ac757455268fa4c51c40c834811d8af2f91dfbce93ee21df1af" :answer/id #uuid "b22a3ccf-95a1-5e42-ae56-251a25a3e126" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79210/selection/60257a7214ff0337c366dfd312ad9bb8b671bdace6817615348b0092c556332c" :answer/id #uuid "9a1ba595-03f3-5b76-aa2f-d2d15ded6142" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79210/selection/7603e3ded2eb180b05378773fafced67dbb02d079c17ea98edac3ba0f118efdf" :answer/id #uuid "e2eb507b-780a-5a44-b515-fd429e115ae0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79210/selection/96a865d4bb28ed6e96fc940867a37e6e9671c39a2eb0ac7f0dbfea5e98e4119e" :answer/id #uuid "80b00e7b-a374-5784-8dea-d1e04d4c65d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0,\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79210/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ace54cc-89fa-5885-8314-306137ac2df5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79210/selection/7603e3ded2eb180b05378773fafced67dbb02d079c17ea98edac3ba0f118efdf" :answer-field/choices ["q-79210/selection/9d807895a62ccaaa6b0f7870d94a0d6b4337ff0af504bf4b2897ef85a13c3072" "q-79210/selection/25eef7f15a6e5ac757455268fa4c51c40c834811d8af2f91dfbce93ee21df1af" "q-79210/selection/60257a7214ff0337c366dfd312ad9bb8b671bdace6817615348b0092c556332c" "q-79210/selection/7603e3ded2eb180b05378773fafced67dbb02d079c17ea98edac3ba0f118efdf" "q-79210/selection/96a865d4bb28ed6e96fc940867a37e6e9671c39a2eb0ac7f0dbfea5e98e4119e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79210" :question/id #uuid "deee9493-d2a1-55ca-a638-e9ba7b8a8aab" :question/math-academy-id "q-79210" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/4d98583cd4066bc79695d9ceb75393d80c3a341e1a44c76a2144058da79bb5ca.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(5,-4).$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the circle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/b71fec9561c150627ca6cfce06e8e79a9fa77f9395930f4bb9cf1a755cf350b2.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-79210/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79210"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-106293/selection/7603e3ded2eb180b05378773fafced67dbb02d079c17ea98edac3ba0f118efdf" :answer/id #uuid "98810e9c-98ff-5a47-8f20-c096d8dfb45b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106293/selection/60257a7214ff0337c366dfd312ad9bb8b671bdace6817615348b0092c556332c" :answer/id #uuid "8bfc5bb3-04ea-5e21-9cd9-c07da5511097" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106293/selection/96a865d4bb28ed6e96fc940867a37e6e9671c39a2eb0ac7f0dbfea5e98e4119e" :answer/id #uuid "18df0079-cebb-5428-9e8d-a99004032963" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0,\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106293/selection/770a95c293bd13db106ebde4bac5b83d71e2424854e76736f00d71578c0befe6" :answer/id #uuid "bb085cfd-76cf-5a9c-880d-ba440ddfa49a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106293/selection/d5415bb83d35d76d1b88a5539a8100f03d693876172db33fec1567cff0512148" :answer/id #uuid "d7febb20-21e6-5bfa-8632-63560adc382b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106293/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "57a9bc68-4e58-5c41-9a23-7c3233e37ee7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-106293/selection/770a95c293bd13db106ebde4bac5b83d71e2424854e76736f00d71578c0befe6" :answer-field/choices ["q-106293/selection/7603e3ded2eb180b05378773fafced67dbb02d079c17ea98edac3ba0f118efdf" "q-106293/selection/60257a7214ff0337c366dfd312ad9bb8b671bdace6817615348b0092c556332c" "q-106293/selection/96a865d4bb28ed6e96fc940867a37e6e9671c39a2eb0ac7f0dbfea5e98e4119e" "q-106293/selection/770a95c293bd13db106ebde4bac5b83d71e2424854e76736f00d71578c0befe6" "q-106293/selection/d5415bb83d35d76d1b88a5539a8100f03d693876172db33fec1567cff0512148"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106293" :question/id #uuid "da600baa-a9d3-5724-9a5b-8920df8b8600" :question/math-academy-id "q-106293" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/d698646baac96e15db7f447c0e1af6dbc0cb03cae50084fbfbb6cab15eabf565.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=2.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/b76043921a3f9399049d6c3b4f557c7263da4d38a070c66a2dd444dbe2806c8e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-106293/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106293"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-78681"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/dd128890a13c0aa79d293b70063eeb8b78e07f56dd42774699d1b2f32e43dd0d.png)\n\nThe semi-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=7.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/25503e83f9a196cab4ded2fd93b09028b1608bd7d815edd538564e1aa611ca86.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the required semi-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-circle are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-321989/field-1/04ab8c59d12921ca2d995f79fd8be713086563c65c468afaa0d4bd8ea5bfffaf" :answer/id #uuid "3e26fb45-f7d8-5be3-a5df-348f36b640f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321989/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b98d5869-8856-5222-8f73-96204957f941" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321989/field-1/04ab8c59d12921ca2d995f79fd8be713086563c65c468afaa0d4bd8ea5bfffaf" :answer-field/choices ["q-321989/field-1/04ab8c59d12921ca2d995f79fd8be713086563c65c468afaa0d4bd8ea5bfffaf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321989/field-2/17f7a92ecac965d3850a7da98658af322c6b691d4b3336f622586133c927298d" :answer/id #uuid "309921f1-1391-5cf9-8d3d-9df75fecd825" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321989/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "80ad2647-3d64-5745-98ba-408af04584d5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321989/field-2/17f7a92ecac965d3850a7da98658af322c6b691d4b3336f622586133c927298d" :answer-field/choices ["q-321989/field-2/17f7a92ecac965d3850a7da98658af322c6b691d4b3336f622586133c927298d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321989/field-3/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "a0a3f17c-7c15-5d72-8903-88097bc938ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321989/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "b0976887-4faf-532f-a123-ca8a545a3158" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321989/field-3/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer-field/choices ["q-321989/field-3/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321989" :question/id #uuid "03d69679-f524-5057-8f75-9945b7cf6ef2" :question/math-academy-id "q-321989" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{7}{2},\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{11}{4},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{7}{2} & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x-\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+\\frac{11}{4} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(y+\\frac{11}{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}=9.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-321989/field-1" "q-321989/field-2" "q-321989/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-321989"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-275313/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "f69f25ed-e82b-551c-8aec-f8b85a84a806" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-275313/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "b7625393-d3a6-537a-82c0-4aa906791400" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-275313/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8764504-3b49-5c0c-b3d1-5d23409093c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-275313/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-275313/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-275313/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-275313/field-2/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "160bdb48-bc18-5f62-b990-4149f5312636" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-275313/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ebb7b00-7da6-5922-973b-4b938af8ea33" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-275313/field-2/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-275313/field-2/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-275313/field-3/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" :answer/id #uuid "c2a0a858-3d2c-54a9-9b6b-5ee29feb84c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-275313/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "9cd9d235-660d-5bbb-bb77-294dbe64aaaa" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-275313/field-3/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" :answer-field/choices ["q-275313/field-3/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-275313" :question/id #uuid "1a9d2783-5a0f-5b83-8cab-893c8a31f08f" :question/math-academy-id "q-275313" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{6} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=36.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-275313/field-1" "q-275313/field-2" "q-275313/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-275313"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-106300/selection/81bd620d0442a19150db3bfb8f7c61b25c33a119533414bba16210daa4414c3d" :answer/id #uuid "5d0c64de-4799-5075-aeb1-e9e4bedcd99c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106300/selection/0a4697ac75f2efc8c154730c7d4c98c526ddc5bb9a657aa0afd784e14d85aa06" :answer/id #uuid "ecd7c91a-9e6f-5864-9f46-854f138238f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106300/selection/397ca070a1b75b6eabc02a090c7218dafe08b027217dcc2c999f22b4db84efcb" :answer/id #uuid "f796e756-fbad-5213-92fe-34e7bc9736b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106300/selection/05451571a21afe0b437eda6d3cf20083f47be0abd79337f2ee11002703c41432" :answer/id #uuid "204541c0-24f8-58f7-85be-e4918313b591" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106300/selection/0c8a7caa06ef045080c07a85e5fc336c25a608e126068eeb440e6dcd54f1086d" :answer/id #uuid "b94103fa-ab6c-57be-bbb3-9db306bb275d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106300/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "11b1e003-da9b-596c-ba40-c91af3644f87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-106300/selection/05451571a21afe0b437eda6d3cf20083f47be0abd79337f2ee11002703c41432" :answer-field/choices ["q-106300/selection/81bd620d0442a19150db3bfb8f7c61b25c33a119533414bba16210daa4414c3d" "q-106300/selection/0a4697ac75f2efc8c154730c7d4c98c526ddc5bb9a657aa0afd784e14d85aa06" "q-106300/selection/397ca070a1b75b6eabc02a090c7218dafe08b027217dcc2c999f22b4db84efcb" "q-106300/selection/05451571a21afe0b437eda6d3cf20083f47be0abd79337f2ee11002703c41432" "q-106300/selection/0c8a7caa06ef045080c07a85e5fc336c25a608e126068eeb440e6dcd54f1086d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106300" :question/id #uuid "d07f0ee1-8739-5256-bf30-c82b7eeddac5" :question/math-academy-id "q-106300" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-106300/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106300"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79221/selection/b1f3bb2d85aed638ad4e0fff6947f991215781e79c8c055b2d91696fccaea1d5" :answer/id #uuid "f2a500cb-13e8-5c35-94fb-220d40d5cd03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79221/selection/2a83b08d6f9390b863cc7b4a1b666cb84f6707eac9d76e492a1d822310779f18" :answer/id #uuid "1fb737d9-6de8-5e97-8000-4ef442ee10f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79221/selection/49ac806ace8fe693b9bd046400092a73cf257a8e3c6d8f29b680a47559a790c9" :answer/id #uuid "26a22685-aa53-52bf-9042-affb9261b909" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79221/selection/57656992e4a79c59736f54df98281a1946de760832afdb74f19b5819bb5a3a81" :answer/id #uuid "8cb053a0-af9a-58ad-953f-75f723563c8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79221/selection/c5c9fd6c3bdaf199b55a3cdbbc4f9c5119eff2dd70466e4adc7d1edbe225b2a5" :answer/id #uuid "f9b156e2-d677-5816-8578-ade01ccf161a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79221/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e7f557df-3792-5d35-9fc6-05c142f12f3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79221/selection/2a83b08d6f9390b863cc7b4a1b666cb84f6707eac9d76e492a1d822310779f18" :answer-field/choices ["q-79221/selection/b1f3bb2d85aed638ad4e0fff6947f991215781e79c8c055b2d91696fccaea1d5" "q-79221/selection/2a83b08d6f9390b863cc7b4a1b666cb84f6707eac9d76e492a1d822310779f18" "q-79221/selection/49ac806ace8fe693b9bd046400092a73cf257a8e3c6d8f29b680a47559a790c9" "q-79221/selection/57656992e4a79c59736f54df98281a1946de760832afdb74f19b5819bb5a3a81" "q-79221/selection/c5c9fd6c3bdaf199b55a3cdbbc4f9c5119eff2dd70466e4adc7d1edbe225b2a5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79221" :question/id #uuid "480062d9-9533-5d48-bc7f-73b46822f0d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79221" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+3}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-79221/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79221"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-15845/selection/5da03679dcb4e7e3e661baa66082767d104f8fa946a569f68aab4224c7ca741d" :answer/id #uuid "c6658429-805d-5158-878a-b40253eab0a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15845/selection/f4403beca24c17e0b20620528e1bae05a28a41946083d2954ad97006465230cf" :answer/id #uuid "71a7ab43-9280-5582-8056-6f4de3d648f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15845/selection/74414952cb62dde9fdbdecdc7b326f8364f0ee3187fe4d34b264aabcbb45a963" :answer/id #uuid "894e7701-f050-5718-bfa4-99ccd8b67b43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15845/selection/47a23fca3f5274fce3231d1847ee7d20d97ab81acac0d68ae17898bc29f99619" :answer/id #uuid "fc043568-6dad-5196-961f-1b7fa1355fb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15845/selection/1a8288f753dc8dc6af60dd2271ef07be775bb158aaa614ebf1310d11a5312e60" :answer/id #uuid "3660fd32-70e6-5f4c-bf98-27c86b4daeb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15845/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa531025-2766-59b6-959b-7553463ec1fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-15845/selection/f4403beca24c17e0b20620528e1bae05a28a41946083d2954ad97006465230cf" :answer-field/choices ["q-15845/selection/5da03679dcb4e7e3e661baa66082767d104f8fa946a569f68aab4224c7ca741d" "q-15845/selection/f4403beca24c17e0b20620528e1bae05a28a41946083d2954ad97006465230cf" "q-15845/selection/74414952cb62dde9fdbdecdc7b326f8364f0ee3187fe4d34b264aabcbb45a963" "q-15845/selection/47a23fca3f5274fce3231d1847ee7d20d97ab81acac0d68ae17898bc29f99619" "q-15845/selection/1a8288f753dc8dc6af60dd2271ef07be775bb158aaa614ebf1310d11a5312e60"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-15845" :question/id #uuid "00d82038-4e0a-5079-b90e-fc5cfcb65e81" :question/math-academy-id "q-15845" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(x-1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(y-2) & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4(y-2)^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the Cartesian equation of the circle is $(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-15845/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-15845"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5374"] :question/problem "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r = 2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5375"] :question/problem "Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that generates a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx= r\\cos\\theta+a,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta+b,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta+4,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta+6,\\quad\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6779"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6817"] :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta + 2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta - 4, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+4}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
